      "end": 35.301458333333336,
      "lesson_progress": 0.2727272727272727,
      "text": "Success criteria. I can name the tenths, hundredths and thousandths places. I can explain a decimal using a place-value chart. I can identify the value of a digit in a decimal number.",
      "chapter": "Success Criteria: I can…",
      "equation": [],
      "board": [
        "1. Name the tenths, hundredths and thousandths places.",
        "2. Explain a decimal using a place-value chart.",
        "3. Identify the value of a digit in a decimal number."
      ],
      "rows": [],
      "line": null,
      "area": null,
      "context": null,
      "note": "",
      "role": "success_criteria"
    },
    {
      "index": 3,
      "start": 35.301458333333336,
      "end": 48.149541666666664,
      "lesson_progress": 0.36363636363636365,
      "text": "To the right of the decimal point come tenths, hundredths and thousandths. Each place is one tenth of the place to its left. A digit gives the number of those units; its position gives their size.",
      "chapter": "Build the idea",
      "equation": [],
      "board": [],
      "rows": [],
      "line": null,
      "area": null,
      "context": null,
      "note": "",
      "role": "explanation"
    },
    {
      "index": 4,
      "start": 48.149541666666664,
      "end": 59.53058333333333,
      "lesson_progress": 0.45454545454545453,
      "text": "Read four point three zero seven. The four represents four ones, the three represents three tenths, and the seven represents seven thousandths. There are no hundredths.",
      "chapter": "Example 1: plan",
      "equation": [],
      "board": [
        "4.307",
        "4 ones + 3 tenths + 0 hundredths + 7 thousandths"
      ],
      "rows": [],
      "line": null,
      "area": null,
      "context": null,
      "note": "",
      "role": "explanation"
    },
    {
      "index": 5,
      "start": 59.53058333333333,
      "end": 68.538125,
      "lesson_progress": 0.5454545454545454,
      "text": "The zero keeps the seven in the thousandths place. Without that zero, four point three seven would have seven hundredths, a different amount.",
      "chapter": "Example 1: explain",
      "equation": [],
      "board": [
        "4.307 ≠ 4.37",
        "7 thousandths = 0.007"
      ],
      "rows": [],
      "line": null,
      "area": null,
      "context": null,
      "note": "",
      "role": "explanation"
    },
    {
      "index": 6,
      "start": 68.538125,
      "end": 76.817625,
      "lesson_progress": 0.6363636363636364,
      "text": "Find the value of the six in twelve point zero six four. The six is the second digit after the decimal point.",
      "chapter": "Example 2: plan",
      "equation": [],
      "board": [
